A||||5||JohnnyK||VERY important question about light bulb.||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||02-02-2001||01:31 PM||||This is so important because I need to get this car certified today or tommorow. It is the front drivers side orange blinker I am talking about (on the bumper, NOT the side marker). It does not turn on when I have the lights on, but it does turn on when I am using that blinker. The passenger side one is on all the time and then flashes when using that blinker. What could be wrong with this? Hopefully not a big electrical problem?||||reg||1
Z||000001||fiero56||02-02-2001||02:03 PM||||Did you pull the bulb and check the elements?||||reg||1
Z||000002||kslish||02-02-2001||02:50 PM||||Provided the bulbs are good, you have a bad ground in the wiring somewhere.  When I was reparing my '86 SC's front end after an accident, my parking lights and turn signals acted the exact same way as you are describing.  The ground had broken off from the passenger side socket due to the blow the front facia took during the accident.<P>Solution: find the bad wire using a wiring tester and repair the break or splice in a new ground someplace.<P>Ken S.<BR>'86 SC and '87 GT<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> <p>[This message has been edited by kslish (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||02-02-2001||04:03 PM||||So if it's flashing for turning, the bulb is obviously good right? There isn't some trick to these bulbs like 2 filaments. one for steady and one for flashing or soemthing? (I dunno, it could happen).  **** , I really can't get in there to check it out. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||02-02-2001||04:09 PM||||There are two filaments. One for "low" and one for "high".<P>It's not that hard to change. Two T-15 bolts and pull the unit out.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 02-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||02-02-2001||05:20 PM||||Yup, 2 filaments. One for park lights and the other for turn signal.  BTW, you brake lights are the same way.  Check the bulb first, before tearing into any wiring.  Possibly a dirty or corroded socket, too.<P>------------------<BR>When you choke a smurf, what color does it turn?||||reg||1
